WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.661
Spark, a program that runs data jobs across many machines, splits its work into pieces called tasks.

2
00:00:09.661 --> 00:00:11.332
Here's a nightly report.

3
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:18.196
Read four files of payments, keep the captured ones, add up the amounts for each shop, and collect the result.

4
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.723
Spark gives each file its own task.

5
00:00:20.723 --> 00:00:23.976
After the grouping, it's set to make four pieces.

6
00:00:23.976 --> 00:00:25.834
How many tasks run in total?

7
00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:26.896
Pause and guess.

8
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:31.823
Hold on to your guess.

9
00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:32.899
Let's cook it.

10
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.505
Start with the files.

11
00:00:34.505 --> 00:00:36.981
Four files, four tasks, one per file.

12
00:00:36.981 --> 00:00:42.869
Each task reads its file, keeps the captured payments, and adds up the amounts per shop.

13
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:47.041
Task two can filter file two without looking at anything else.

14
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:50.216
So the steps run back to back inside one task.

15
00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:55.794
Read a batch of rows, filter it, add it to a running sum per shop, then the next batch.

16
00:00:55.794 --> 00:00:58.167
Nothing in between gets written down.

17
00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:03.265
Steps like these, where each piece needs only its own input, are called narrow.

18
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.006
But here's the catch.

19
00:01:05.006 --> 00:01:07.791
Shop 42 sold things in all four files.

20
00:01:07.791 --> 00:01:12.041
So each task ends up with its own part of shop 42's total.

21
00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:16.429
Four part totals, in four places, and nobody has the real one.

22
00:01:16.429 --> 00:01:22.316
Adding up per shop is a wide step: the answer for one shop needs rows from every piece.

23
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:25.072
A restaurant kitchen has the same problem.

24
00:01:25.072 --> 00:01:26.885
The steak comes off the grill.

25
00:01:26.885 --> 00:01:28.818
The fries come out of the fryer.

26
00:01:28.818 --> 00:01:32.565
Each cook works their own station, and that's the narrow part.

27
00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:39.459
But a plate can't go out until its steak and its fries meet at the pass, the counter where each order comes together.

28
00:01:39.459 --> 00:01:44.321
Nobody wants the fries delivered to table nine while the steak is still on the grill.

29
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.541
Let's map that.

30
00:01:45.541 --> 00:01:47.416
The stations are the tasks.

31
00:01:47.416 --> 00:01:49.083
The pass is the shuffle.

32
00:01:49.083 --> 00:01:51.929
And a finished plate is one shop's total.

33
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.777
Here's how the pass works.

34
00:01:53.777 --> 00:01:59.260
Each task splits its part totals into four parts, one for each task of the next step.

35
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:07.147
It picks the part by hashing the shop number and keeping the remainder after dividing by four, the same trick MapReduce used.

36
00:02:07.147 --> 00:02:09.985
It writes the parts to its own machine's disk.

37
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:14.572
Then each next-step task fetches its part from every earlier task.

38
00:02:14.572 --> 00:02:16.708
Four times four, sixteen blocks.

39
00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:21.801
All four pieces of shop 42 now meet in one task, which adds them up.

40
00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:23.371
And notice the wait.

41
00:02:23.371 --> 00:02:28.789
No task after the pass can start until every task before it has finished writing.

42
00:02:28.789 --> 00:02:31.130
A plate waits for its slowest dish.

43
00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.537
So the shuffle cuts the work in two.

44
00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:35.155
Now the three words.

45
00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:38.949
A job is everything one action runs; here, collect.

46
00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:42.575
A stage is a stretch of narrow steps between shuffles.

47
00:02:42.575 --> 00:02:46.144
And a task is one piece of data going through one stage.

48
00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:47.411
Count them.

49
00:02:47.411 --> 00:02:48.171
One job.

50
00:02:48.171 --> 00:02:49.215
Two stages.

51
00:02:49.215 --> 00:02:51.874
Four tasks, plus four tasks.

52
00:02:51.874 --> 00:02:52.444
Eight.

53
00:02:52.444 --> 00:02:54.343
Did you guess eight?

54
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.090
So the rule: stages are the number of shuffles plus one.

55
00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:01.873
Tasks are the input pieces plus the pieces of each shuffle.

56
00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:04.113
Spark's plan shows the same thing.

57
00:03:04.113 --> 00:03:08.565
Here's the report in Kotlin, and the plan it prints, read from the bottom.

58
00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:11.314
Everything marked one runs in stage one.

59
00:03:11.314 --> 00:03:13.894
Everything marked two runs in stage two.

60
00:03:13.894 --> 00:03:18.087
And between them sits the shuffle, which the plan calls Exchange.

61
00:03:18.087 --> 00:03:20.216
Count the Exchanges, and add one.

62
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.287
Two things change the count.

63
00:03:22.287 --> 00:03:27.006
The number of pieces after a shuffle is a setting, shuffle partitions.

64
00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:28.354
It's 200 by default.

65
00:03:28.354 --> 00:03:30.645
The lesson's demo sets it to four.

66
00:03:30.833 --> 00:03:36.362
And since Spark 3.2, a feature called adaptive query execution is on by default.

67
00:03:36.362 --> 00:03:45.140
It runs each stage that ends in a shuffle as a job of its own, sees how small the shuffle really was, and plans the rest again.

68
00:03:45.333 --> 00:03:51.164
In the demo, it merges the four tiny pieces into one: two jobs, and five tasks.

69
00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:52.337
One more thing.

70
00:03:52.337 --> 00:03:56.023
The shuffle files sit on one machine's disk, in one copy.

71
00:03:56.023 --> 00:03:58.545
So what happens when that machine dies?

72
00:03:58.545 --> 00:04:00.097
That's the next section.

73
00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:03.480
The moral: narrow steps stay at their own station.

74
00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.621
A wide step sends everything to the pass, and the pass cuts the job into stages.

75
00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.265
One quick check.

76
00:04:10.265 --> 00:04:15.432
A plan reads four files and has two Exchanges, each making four pieces.

77
00:04:15.432 --> 00:04:18.053
How many stages, and how many tasks?

78
00:04:18.053 --> 00:04:19.217
Pause and think.

79
00:04:22.433 --> 00:04:23.479
Three stages.

80
00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:26.777
Four, plus four, plus four: twelve tasks.
